<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>@ViewData["Title"] - Product Catalog</title>
    @* "~/" means "the root of the website" (the wwwroot folder). It becomes href="/css/site.css".
       It still works if the site is hosted under a sub-folder like /shop/, unlike a hard-coded "/css/...". *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/site.css" />
</head>
<body>
    @* <partial name="..."> is a Tag Helper too: it pastes in the HTML of Views/Shared/_Header.cshtml.
       Same as @await Html.PartialAsync("_Header"), just written like HTML. *@
    <partial name="_Header" />
    <partial name="_Navigation" />
    <main>
        @*
            TempData["SuccessMessage"] is SET in ProductsController (Create and Edit) right before a redirect,
            and READ here. It's in the layout (not one view) so the message shows on whatever page we land on.

            How TempData works:
              1. Controller: TempData["SuccessMessage"] = "..."; return RedirectToAction(...);
              2. The value is saved (in a cookie) and the browser follows the redirect - a brand-new request.
              3. Here we read it once. Reading it marks it for deletion, so refreshing the page
                 does NOT show the message again.
            Why not ViewBag? ViewBag only lives for the current request, so it's gone after the redirect.
            Why redirect at all? So pressing F5 after saving doesn't post the form twice (Post/Redirect/Get).
            "is string successMessage" checks it exists AND is a string, then gives it a name - no message, no box.
        *@
        @if (TempData["SuccessMessage"] is string successMessage)
        {
            <div class="success-alert" id="success-alert">@successMessage</div>
        }
        @RenderBody()
    </main>
    <partial name="_Footer" />
</body>
</html>
